    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This introduction section discusses the profound effect of the family environment on children's body image and eating behavior, highlighting how caregiver messages about food restriction, pressure to eat, and weight loss are linked to children's body attitudes and eating behaviors. The authors note a gap in research regarding the long-term impact of these messages beyond adolescence and propose two studies to develop a measure of perceived caregiver eating messages and investigate its psychometric properties. The section also outlines a theoretical model and hypotheses concerning the associations between caregiver messages, body attitudes (including body appreciation and body dissatisfaction), and eating behaviors (intuitive eating and disordered eating), and explores potential gender differences.",
